WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:06.310
Fieldnote three.

2
00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:08.320
The SPI boundary.

3
00:00:09.346 --> 00:00:12.436
Where does shared code hand the
lookup to the Azure provider?

4
00:00:13.828 --> 00:00:16.158
The provider lives inside the service.

5
00:00:16.308 --> 00:00:19.038
Open the partition
service from lesson one.

6
00:00:20.468 --> 00:00:25.758
Inside it, a Service Provider Interface
connects shared code to an implementation.

7
00:00:25.988 --> 00:00:30.828
The community implementation and the Azure
implementation connect that interface to

8
00:00:30.828 --> 00:00:32.398
different dependencies.

9
00:00:34.632 --> 00:00:37.192
Common service code calls
a provider interface:

10
00:00:37.192 --> 00:00:42.792
getPartition. The Azure implementation
behind it checks Redis inside

11
00:00:42.792 --> 00:00:45.282
AKS, with middleware credentials.

12
00:00:45.672 --> 00:00:50.152
On a miss, it reads common
Table Storage outside AKS,

13
00:00:50.152 --> 00:00:51.602
with Workload Identity.

14
00:00:51.992 --> 00:00:55.602
And the table read still
happens when the cache throws.

15
00:00:58.152 --> 00:01:01.522
The interface and its
implementation ship in one image.

16
00:01:01.992 --> 00:01:04.632
The Azure module depends
on partition-core,

17
00:01:04.632 --> 00:01:09.202
the shared code, and the build
packs both into one executable JAR.

18
00:01:09.592 --> 00:01:11.602
The image copies that JAR in.

19
00:01:12.152 --> 00:01:15.922
So crossing the interface is a
method call inside one process.

20
00:01:16.152 --> 00:01:18.082
There is no network hop between them.

21
00:01:20.648 --> 00:01:24.978
The fork maintains the Azure provider
separately from generated shared code.

22
00:01:25.128 --> 00:01:28.578
The shared code is regenerated
from the community repository.

23
00:01:28.808 --> 00:01:31.768
The Azure provider stays fork-owned.

24
00:01:31.768 --> 00:01:34.808
So when upstream removes
its own Azure copy,

25
00:01:34.808 --> 00:01:39.138
as it plans to, the fork's
provider stays where it is.

26
00:01:41.656 --> 00:01:45.106
Follow the same lookup from lesson
one, now through the provider.

27
00:01:45.416 --> 00:01:48.096
The request for opendes
reaches common code,

28
00:01:48.096 --> 00:01:51.016
which checks the caller,
then calls the interface.

29
00:01:51.016 --> 00:01:56.866
No network hop. The Azure implementation
checks the Redis cache inside AKS.

30
00:01:57.256 --> 00:02:01.056
On a healthy miss, it reads
Table Storage outside AKS,

31
00:02:01.056 --> 00:02:02.386
which returns opendes.

32
00:02:02.776 --> 00:02:04.466
Now take the cache down.

33
00:02:04.856 --> 00:02:08.136
The cache read throws,
safeGet treats it as a miss,

34
00:02:08.136 --> 00:02:10.226
and Table Storage answers anyway.

35
00:02:10.776 --> 00:02:14.216
This lookup never touches
the partition's Cosmos DB,

36
00:02:14.216 --> 00:02:16.386
blob Storage, or Service Bus.

37
00:02:18.812 --> 00:02:20.132
An easy mistake:

38
00:02:20.132 --> 00:02:23.502
the token was accepted, so
the call is authorized.

39
00:02:23.732 --> 00:02:27.902
The sidecar validates the token
against the configured Entra issuers.

40
00:02:28.052 --> 00:02:29.742
That is authentication.

41
00:02:29.892 --> 00:02:32.302
The service still decides authorization.

42
00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:35.502
For most services, that is
an entitlements lookup.

43
00:02:35.732 --> 00:02:39.182
The partition service admits
app-only callers instead.

44
00:02:39.412 --> 00:02:43.822
A 401 or 403 alone does not
say which boundary failed.

45
00:02:43.972 --> 00:02:49.102
To check, read the request
authentication in the OSDU namespace.

46
00:02:51.384 --> 00:02:52.594
So now you can say:

47
00:02:52.744 --> 00:02:55.874
common code calls Azure
through a provider interface.

48
00:02:56.024 --> 00:02:59.074
The interface and implementation
ship in one image.

49
00:02:59.224 --> 00:03:02.674
And the fork keeps Azure source
outside the generated tree.

50
00:03:05.288 --> 00:03:10.818
Next: who owns that provider code, and
how does it survive upstream deleting it?

